WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.133 --> 00:00:04.700
And use case #2
is to use the BigQuery

00:00:04.700 --> 00:00:06.866
for signature based search

00:00:06.866 --> 00:00:10.033
based on the result you got
from the data model.

00:00:10.033 --> 00:00:12.500
You're trying to use
the machine learning engine.

00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:17.066
We use the ML engine for
extracting the feature vectors.

00:00:17.066 --> 00:00:19.266
So I'd like to show
another demonstration

00:00:19.266 --> 00:00:20.766
of QueryIt Smart.

00:00:24.633 --> 00:00:26.033
So if you go to QueryIt Smart,

00:00:26.033 --> 00:00:30.600
you have another demonstration
called "image search."

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.266
And you can choose one
of the key images.

00:00:33.266 --> 00:00:36.000
Let me try with the elephant.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:38.200
So we're gonna be using
this elephant image

00:00:38.200 --> 00:00:39.733
as a key image

00:00:39.733 --> 00:00:43.100
and compare 1 million images
with this image

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:48.866
to find the similar images
on BigQuery.

00:00:48.866 --> 00:00:51.233
And we got it.

00:00:51.233 --> 00:00:55.666
AndBigQuery has processed
8.8 gigabit

00:00:55.666 --> 00:00:59.900
of data over 1 million images
in 10 seconds,

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:02.233
and all you see
is the similar images.

00:01:02.233 --> 00:01:04.933
And you may notice that

00:01:04.933 --> 00:01:07.433
you also have the images
that is not elephant.

00:01:07.433 --> 00:01:08.466
Like this.

00:01:08.466 --> 00:01:10.033
This is not an elephant,

00:01:10.033 --> 00:01:12.533
but still it looks same.

00:01:12.533 --> 00:01:15.433
It looks similar
to the neural networks.

00:01:15.433 --> 00:01:18.233
And this apparently
is not an animal.

00:01:18.233 --> 00:01:21.233
It's a statue, but still
the neural network thinks

00:01:21.233 --> 00:01:24.433
it must be similar thing.

00:01:24.433 --> 00:01:26.333
This is not an elephant,

00:01:26.333 --> 00:01:30.000
but it has the same kind of
the texture of patterns,

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:33.166
so the neural network thinks
they are similar.

00:01:33.166 --> 00:01:35.000
So this is not a keyword search

00:01:35.000 --> 00:01:36.400
or pattern matching,

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.333
but they are comparing the--
all the feature look just--

00:01:39.333 --> 00:01:41.600
or signatures of the images.

00:01:41.600 --> 00:01:46.466
So you can get the much smarter
search on the BigQuery.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:51.100
So how do you do this?

00:01:51.100 --> 00:01:54.000
At first, we have imported
all the 1 million images

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:56.500
from Wikimedia
on the Google Cloud Storage.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:59.133
And then we have used
machine learning engine--

00:01:59.133 --> 00:02:03.166
ML Engine to learn the
convolution on neural networks

00:02:03.166 --> 00:02:04.266
on the ML Engine.

00:02:04.266 --> 00:02:08.400
This is a typical neural network
design

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:11.266
used while analyzing the images.

00:02:11.266 --> 00:02:13.633
But usually people are using CNN

00:02:13.633 --> 00:02:15.566
to get the labels
for the images,

00:02:15.566 --> 00:02:16.833
such as "is it a cat?

00:02:16.833 --> 00:02:19.166
"Is it a human face?
Is it flower?"

00:02:19.166 --> 00:02:21.066
But instead of getting labels,

00:02:21.066 --> 00:02:24.733
we are using CNN to extract
the feature vectors

00:02:24.733 --> 00:02:28.733
that could be in signatures
of our images.

00:02:28.733 --> 00:02:31.266
And by using the BigQuery
and UDF,

00:02:31.266 --> 00:02:34.700
you can do the similarity search
against images.

00:02:34.700 --> 00:02:39.066
And all--we have used VGG16,
which is a popular CNN design,

00:02:39.066 --> 00:02:42.433
and we are extracting
the feature vectors

00:02:42.433 --> 00:02:45.666
from the final fully
connectional layers.

